 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "LLANBERIS. Croeso i Lanberis. Welcome! Population: some people and a great many sheep.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "EAST: Nant Ffrancon, Llyn Ogwen, Tryfan. Dwyrain. Bring chocolate.",
   "x": 60.0,
   "y": 17.0
  },
  {
   "text": "YR WYDDFA (SNOWDON). 1,085 m. Walk, or ride the little train. The mountain does not mind. It has seen it all.",
   "x": 57.0,
   "y": 4.0
  },
  {
   "text": "SOUTH: Cambrian Coast. Y De. Please do not feed the seagulls. They have a union too.",
   "x": 25.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "local": {
    "cy": "Chwarel Dinorwig"
   },
   "text": "DINORWIC QUARRY. 1787 to 1969. Hard hats provided. Hard heads optional.",
   "x": 16.0,
   "y": 17.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ELECTRIC MOUNTAIN. A power station inside a mountain. Wales: 'we put the lights on, under a hill'.",
   "x": 43.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "local": {
    "cy": "Castell Dolbadarn"
   },
   "text": "DOLBADARN CASTLE. 13th century. One round tower, a lot of Welsh princes, no roof. Still standing.",
   "x": 8.0,
   "y": 26.0
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Dinorwic Quarry employed up to 3,000 men at its height. It closed in 1969.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "llanberis_dinorwic",
   "title": "Dinorwic Quarry"
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 6,
   "plaque": "llanberis_name",
   "title": "Name: Llanberis",
   "text": "Llan in a Welsh place name means a church enclosure, and Llanberis is the llan of Peris, an early Welsh saint. His old church still stands up the pass at Nant Peris, with a holy well nearby. There are hundreds of llans in Wales, so the saints were clearly busy."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 16,
   "local": {
    "cy": "Amgueddfa Lechi Cymru"
   },
   "plaque": "llanberis_slate_museum",
   "title": "The Slate Museum",
   "text": "The quarry's old workshops at Gilfach Ddu are now the National Slate Museum. Its waterwheel, over 15 metres across, once drove the machinery for the whole works and is the largest on mainland Britain. Watch a craftsman split slate thinner than a biscuit."
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "llanberis_ceunant_mawr",
   "title": "Ceunant Mawr",
   "text": "Just above the village, a stream tumbles down Ceunant Mawr, the 'great ravine', in a waterfall that visitors have admired since Victorian times. The Snowdon Mountain Railway crosses the gorge on a stone viaduct, so passengers get a free look on the way up."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 17,
   "plaque": "llanberis_cymraeg",
   "title": "Cymraeg",
   "text": "Gwynedd has the highest share of Welsh speakers of any county in Wales, so in Llanberis you will hear plenty of Cymraeg, the Welsh language, in the shop queue. Try diolch for thank you, and iechyd da, good health, for when the tea arrives."
  }
 ],
